Every one of the 4,552 multi-user configurations submitted to the joint evaluator was valid and emitted; none was silently dropped. Per-anchor catalogue sizes ranged from 961 to 1,008.

### Joint evaluation path

For each anchor, one fresh nominal `StepEvaluator` with `boundary_indices=(0,)` received the canonical default configuration and every multi-user catalogue configuration in one `evaluate_many` call. No singleton configuration was sent to an evaluator. This matches the stored physics header’s declared current candidate/default path. Forecasts were not regenerated because C3 needs the immediate `Delta F(A)` only; the already-stored C2 forecast quantities were not changed.

The stored unilateral term for each member was computed from its authenticated stored singleton and stored default. The new joint term was computed from the jointly evaluated configuration and the fresh default from that same joint batch. Thus neither `d_i` nor `Delta F(A)` mixes scalar/dense paths or boundary policies. Both use nominal `evaluate_many` at boundary `[0]`.

A relevant implementation fact was exposed: a fresh default evaluated on the joint batch was not always binary-identical to the default stored from the original default-plus-singletons batch. The dense engine occupancy-sorts configurations and processes chunks ([run_v025_matrix_probe.py evaluator entry](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-coalgen-ws/.scratch/multi-catfish-v025-physics-successor/probe/run_v025_matrix_probe.py:793), [batch sorting/chunking](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-c1c2suff-ws/src/mcrl/physics_v025/batch.py:159)). The build therefore did not substitute either default across batches: stored `d_i` and fresh `Delta F(A)` each retain their own internally consistent default evaluation, and `Psi_A` is formed as the requested difference of those two measured increments.

### Required C3 schema

The runner first authenticates and validates canonical exact-source JSONL ([run_stagec_training.py:217](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/scripts/run_stagec_training.py:217)); it inventories only `views` paths ([lines 274–288](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/scripts/run_stagec_training.py:274)); then it dispatches exact C1/C2 and C3 schemas, requires both families, and requires one common physics digest ([lines 291–326](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/scripts/run_stagec_training.py:291)).

The exact C3 row fields were taken from the `CoalitionRow` declaration at [coalitions.py:290](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/coalitions.py:290). Its constructor requires TRAIN rows of size at least two, the complete changed-user inventory, calibrated float-hex prices, authority digests, and both reconstruction identities ([lines 325–383](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/coalitions.py:325)). The writer’s canonical JSONL header and sidecar path are at [lines 556–579](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/coalitions.py:556), and the reader checks header equality, canonical bytes, exact row fields, row digests, and context digests at [lines 709–774](/home/sat/mcrl-v025-retrain-ws/src/mcrl/stagec_v025/coalitions.py:709).

All emitted C3 rows carry physics digest `38e1998aa8c1f4a751bca5456ec7e900feed16c52d38504bb76d28b556130b55`, exactly the digest carried by the copied C1/C2 views.

